L000244	\emph{Pion $\beta$ decay}---The master formula for pion $\beta$ decay reads~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng,Czarnecki:2019iwz}\footnote{$G_F$ can be identified with its value extracted from muon decay $G_F=1.1663785(6)\times 10^{-5}\GeV^{-2}$~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2026,MuLan:2012sih}, since the only process-dependent corrections not accounted for via the conventions~\eqref{eq:LEFT1} concern mass corrections to the $W$ propagator $2\Delta G_F^W\simeq 3m_\mu^2/(5M_W^2)\simeq 1\times 10^{-6}$~\cite{Ferroglia:2013dga,Fael:2013pja}.}
L000245	\begin{align}
L000246	\Gamma\big[\pi^+\to\pi^0 e^+\nu_e(\gamma)\big]&=
L000247	\frac{G_F^2|V_{ud}|^2\mpi^5|f_+^\pi(0)|^2}{64\pi^3}\notag\\
L000248	&\times\big(1+\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}\big)I_{\pi\ell},
L000249	\end{align}
L000250	where $\mpi$ denotes the mass of the charged pion. The form factor $f_+^\pi(0)\simeq 1-7\times 10^{-6}$ deviates from unity by a negligible amount due to the Behrends--Sirlin--Ademollo--Gatto theorem~\cite{Behrends:1960nf,Ademollo:1964sr,Gasser:1984ux}, which ensures that IB corrections can only enter quadratically. The phase-space factor evaluates to
L000251	\beq
L000252	\label{phase_space}
L000253	I_{\pi\ell}=7.3767(41)\times 10^{-8},
L000254	\eeq
L000255	where the uncertainty derives from the pion mass difference, $\mpi-M_{\pi^0}=4.59364(48)\MeV$~\cite{Crawford:1990jc}.\footnote{This value includes corrections from the slope parameter $\lambda_+=0.036(3)$~\cite{Gasser:1984ux}, with uncertainties derived from higher-order effects in the LEC $L_6^r(M_\rho)=6.6(6)\times 10^{-3}$~\cite{Gasser:1984gg,Bijnens:2002hp,Colangelo:2021moe}.} Radiative corrections are included in $\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}$, which decomposes as
L000256	\beq
L000257	\label{Delta_RC_def}
L000258	\frac{1+\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}}{\big[1+\Delta \tilde S_\text{EW}(\mu_\chi)\big]^2}
L000259	=1+
L000260	\frac{3\alpha_\chi(\mu_\chi)}{2\pi}\log\frac{\mu_\chi}{2E_0}+\Delta I_{\pi\ell},
L000261	\eeq
L000262	where $\Delta I_{\pi\ell}=-0.00378(8)$~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng}, including an estimate of higher chiral orders $\simeq\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\big(\frac{\mpi}{M_\rho}\big)^2\simeq 7.5\times 10^{-5}$.
L000263	In this form, we isolated the only large logarithm that remains in
L000264	 the phase-space correction $\Delta I_{\pi\ell}$ by virtue of the Kinoshita--Lee--Nauenberg theorem~\cite{Passera:2011ae,Kinoshita:1962ur,Lee:1964is}, sensitive to the endpoint energy
L000265	$E_0=(\mpi^2-M_{\pi^0}^2+m_e^2)/(2\mpi)=4.51894(48)\MeV$.
L000266	While we use the full relativistic calculation from Ref.~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng}, the value of $\Delta I_{\pi\ell}$ can be understood from the integral over the Sirlin function, see End Matter.
L000267	Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:final_res}, we find
L000268	for the effective $\pi \pi$ vector coupling
L000269	\begin{equation}
L000270	\label{gVpi_Mrho}
L000271	    g_V^\pi (M_\rho)  = 1.01084(2)_{\bar \Box_\pi} (3)_{\mu}[4]_\text{tot},
L000272	\end{equation}
L000273	where the first uncertainty is propagated from Eq.~\eqref{lattice_box}\footnote{For Ref.~\cite{Yoo:2023gln}, we use the four-flavor analog of Eq.~\eqref{eq:final_res}~\cite{Supp}, yielding a $g_V^\pi(M_\rho)$ that only differs by $3 \times 10^{-6}$ from the three-flavor result derived from Ref.~\cite{Feng:2020zdc}. Accordingly, we quote the average as our final value.} and the second one indicates the residual perturbative uncertainty estimated by varying the  decoupling and matching scales as in Ref.~\cite{Gorbahn:2025ssv}.
L000274	For the radiative-correction factor $\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}$ we further need to consider the evolution to $\mu_\chi\simeq 2E_0$, which gives
L000275	\begin{equation}
L000276	\label{Delta_RC}
L000277	\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell} = 0.03403(4)_{\bar \Box_\pi} (5)_{\mu} (5)_{\mu_\chi}(8)_\text{ChPT}[11]_\text{tot},
L000278	\end{equation}
L000279	where the uncertainty denoted by $\mu_\chi$ comprises a variation of $\mu_\chi$ within $[2E_0/\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{2}\, 2E_0]$ and of the matching scale within $[M_\rho/\sqrt{2},\sqrt{2}M_\rho]$, dominated by the latter.
L000280	
L000281	
L000282	Our result~\eqref{Delta_RC} differs substantially from $\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}|_\text{\cite{Feng:2020zdc}}=0.0332(1)_{\bar \Box_\pi}(3)_\text{QED}$, primarily due to the low-energy RG corrections and the quadratic terms in Eq.~\eqref{Delta_RC_def}. In a fully linearized form,
L000283	the direct application of Eq.~\eqref{gVpi_Mrho} would give $\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}=0.0335$, which then increases by
L000284	\begin{align}
L000285	\Delta_\text{RC}^{\pi\ell}\big|_\text{LL+quad.}&\simeq \bigg(\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\bigg)^2\bigg[\frac{17}{16}\log^2\frac{M_\rho}{2E_0}\\
L000286	&+\bigg(\log\frac{M_Z}{M_\rho}+\frac{3}{4}\log\frac{M_\rho}{2E_0}\bigg)^2\bigg]
L000287	\simeq 4.7\times 10^{-4}\notag
L000288	\end{align}
L000289	due to LL and quadratic terms.
L000290	The final result in Eq.~\eqref{Delta_RC} is obtained from the full RG solution, including another $\simeq 0.6\times 10^{-4}$ from NLL.
