L000301	\emph{$\tau\to\pi\pi\nu_\tau$}---The photon-inclusive  $\tau\to\pi\pi\nu_\tau$ decay rate is expressed as
L000302	\begin{equation}
L000303	\frac{d\Gamma\big[\tau^\pm\to\pi^\pm\pi^0\nu_\tau(\gamma)\big]}{K_\Gamma(s)ds}=\big[\beta_{\pi\pi^0}(s)\big]^3|f_+(s)|^2 S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}G_\text{EM}(s),
L000304	\label{decay_rate}
L000305	\end{equation}
L000306	where
L000307	\beq
L000308	K_\Gamma(s)= \frac{\Gamma_e \vert V_{ud}\vert^2}{2 m_\tau^2} \bigg(1-\frac{s}{m_\tau^2}\bigg)^2\bigg(1+\frac{2s}{m_\tau^2}\bigg),
L000309	\eeq
L000310	$\Gamma_e\equiv \Gamma[\tau\to e\nu_\tau\bar{\nu}_e]$, $\beta_{\pi\pi^0}$ denotes the phase-space factor
L000311	\begin{align}
L000312	\beta_{\pi\pi^0}(s)&=\lambda^{1/2}\bigg(1,\frac{\mpi^2}{s},\frac{M_{\pi^0}^2}{s}\bigg),\notag\\ \lambda(a,b,c)&=a^2+b^2+c^2-2(ab+ac+bc),
L000313	\end{align}
L000314	and $S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}$~\cite{Sirlin:1981ie,Marciano:1985pd,Marciano:1988vm,Marciano:1993sh,Braaten:1990ef,Erler:2002mv,Davier:2002dy} includes short-distance effects.
L000315	The rate is normalized to
L000316	the leptonic decay width $\Gamma_e$, which requires that the respective radiative corrections $\Delta S_\text{EW}^e=\frac{\alpha}{2\pi}\big(\frac{25}{4}-\pi^2\big)$ that connect it to $G_F$ be removed
L000317	\beq
L000318	\label{SEW}
L000319	S^{\pi\pi}_\text{EW}= \big[1+\Delta\tilde S_\text{EW}(\mu_\chi)\big]^2-\Delta S_\text{EW}^{e}.
L000320	\eeq
L000321	Besides providing a convenient normalization channel, this convention also avoids mass corrections $\simeq m_\tau^2/M_W^2\simeq 5\times 10^{-4}$~\cite{Ferroglia:2013dga,Fael:2013pja}, as would arise when using $G_F$ from muon decay instead. The chiral LECs are contained in $G_\text{EM}(s)$, depending linearly on $X_\ell(\mu_\chi)$~\cite{Colangelo:2025ivq}
L000322	\beq
L000323	\label{GEM_Xell}
L000324	   G_\text{EM}(s)\big|_{X_\ell(\mu_\chi)=0}
L000325	= G_\text{EM}(s) \big|_{X_\ell(\mu_\chi) = \bar{X}_\ell(\mu_\chi)} + e^2 \bar{X}_\ell(\mu_\chi).
L000326	\eeq
L000327	The analysis of $G_\text{EM}(s)$ in
L000328	Refs.~\cite{Colangelo:2025iad,Colangelo:2025ivq} is performed for the reference value $\bar{X}_\ell(M_\rho)=14\times 10^{-3}$~\cite{Ma:2021azh}, which can be easily adjusted to an arbitrary value by means of Eq.~\eqref{GEM_Xell}. It is evident that only the product $S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}G_\text{EM}(s)$ is scale and scheme independent, and in the following we will therefore only consider the combined effect.
L000329	Since both $S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}$ and the relevant terms in $G_\text{EM}(s)$ do not depend on the $\pi\pi$ invariant mass $s$, the respective IB corrections for the HVP contribution to $a_\mu$ can simply be obtained by scaling $\Delta \bar a_\mu[S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}]=-12.166(56)(8)\times 10^{-10}$ accordingly,\footnote{Quantities taken from Refs.~\cite{Colangelo:2025iad,Colangelo:2025ivq} are indicated by a bar.} so that the analysis of Refs.~\cite{Colangelo:2025iad,Colangelo:2025ivq} can be updated via
L000330	\begin{align}
L000331	  &\Delta a_\mu[\pi\pi,\tau]-\Delta \bar a_\mu[\pi\pi,\tau]\notag\\
L000332	  &=
L000333	  \Big[\frac{\Delta S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}-\Delta \bar G_\text{EM}}{\Delta \bar S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}}-1\Big]\Delta \bar a_\mu[S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}]\notag\\
L000334	  &=-0.07(4)\times 10^{-10},
L000335	\end{align}
L000336	where $\Delta S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}=S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}-1$ from Eq.~\eqref{SEW}, $\Delta \bar S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}=0.0233$, and
L000337	\beq
L000338	\Delta \bar G_\text{EM}=-e^2\bigg(\bar X_\ell(M_\rho)-\frac{1}{4\pi^2}\log\frac{m_\tau^2}{M_\rho^2}\bigg).
L000339	\eeq
L000340	Including phase-space corrections in addition to $S_\text{EW}^{\pi\pi}$ and $G_\text{EM}(s)$, we obtain
L000341	\beq
L000342	\Delta a_\mu[\pi\pi,\tau]=-24.9(1)_\text{exp}(5)_\text{th}(1)_\text{SD}\times 10^{-10},
L000343	\eeq
L000344	with central value almost identical to Refs.~\cite{Colangelo:2025iad,Colangelo:2025ivq}, but the uncertainty from the short-distance matching, previously estimated as $1.3\times 10^{-10}$~\cite{Aliberti:2025beg},  has become negligible. Accordingly, the uncertainty of isospin breaking in the matrix elements, $|F_\pi^V/f_+|$, estimated as $4.7\times 10^{-10}$ in Ref.~\cite{Aliberti:2025beg}, now constitutes by far the dominant limitation for the use of hadronic $\tau$ data in the evaluation of the HVP contribution to $a_\mu$.
