L000358	\section{End Matter}
L000359	
L000360	\emph{Derivation of the matching conditions and universality}---We arrived at  the matching conditions~\eqref{eq:final_res0} and~\eqref{eq:final_res}
L000361	between LEFT and ChPT in two ways:
L000362	first, following Refs.~\cite{Descotes-Genon:2005wrq,Cirigliano:2023fnz} we  performed the matching at the level of
L000363	Green's functions obtained by  differentiation of the generating functional with respect to charge and weak spurions.
L000364	The results could not be immediately read off of Ref.~\cite{Descotes-Genon:2005wrq}, because for the LEFT
L000365	a Pauli--Villars regulator with on-shell subtraction scheme was used, while
L000366	 we work in dimensional regularization and $\overline{\text{MS}}$ subtraction scheme (to resum logarithms to $\text{NLL}_s$ accuracy).
L000367	Following the logic of  Ref.~\cite{Descotes-Genon:2005wrq},   we arrived at an expression for  $X_\ell(\mu_\chi,\mu,a)$
L000368	in terms of the correlation function   $\int dx\, e^{i k\cdot x}  \langle 0 | T \big\{V_\mu^a (x) V_\nu^b (0)\big\} | \pi^c \rangle$~\cite{Supp},
L000369	where $a,b,c$ are
L000370	SU(3) flavor indices.
L000371	Finally, using a  soft-pion theorem we  related  this matrix element to the object appearing in  the $\gamma W$ box,
L000372	namely $\int dx\, e^{i k\cdot x}  \langle  \pi^d | T \big\{V_\mu^a (x) A_\nu^b (0)\big\} | \pi^c \rangle$, up to higher-order chiral corrections.
L000373	One of the advantages of the  spurion method  is that it allows one to derive representations for any individual LEC,
L000374	and hence the combination  $X_\ell$.
L000375	
L000376	Second, we matched physical amplitudes
L000377	for $\pi^+ \to \pi^0 e^+ \nu_e$ in LEFT and ChPT~\cite{Supp}.
L000378	The key point is that the $\Order(\alpha)$ term in the matrix element $\langle f | O_\beta | i \rangle$  in LEFT
L000379	can be written in terms of convolutions of known kernels
L000380	with  nonperturbative matrix elements of  weak and electromagnetic quark currents between hadronic states.
L000381	Current algebra relates three-point and two-point correlation functions~\cite{Abers:1968zz,Sirlin:1977sv}.
L000382	Inspection of the IR behavior of these convolution integrals~\cite{Abers:1968zz,Sirlin:1967zz}
L000383	allows one to identify the parts of the LEFT amplitude that are  contained in the ChPT loops,
L000384	leaving the rest as contributions to the LECs.
L000385	While this  analysis gives us access only to the linear combination of LECs contributing to the decay rate,
L000386	it  reveals   universal features of the matching:
L000387	Eq.~\eqref{eq:final_res}  formally applies to the  Fermi component of any $\beta$ decay,
L000388	provided one computes the $T_3$ form factor appearing in
L000389	$\bar\Box^V (\mu_0)$, as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:box1}, in the appropriate initial and final state.
L000390	Up to effects suppressed by $E_e/\Mhad$, $m_e/\Mhad$, where $\Mhad$ denotes
L000391	the mass of the decaying hadronic  state
L000392	and $m_e$, $E_e$ mass and energy of the electron, the long-distance corrections
L000393	are also universal (independent of the mass and spin of the hadrons involved in the decay) and provided
L000394	by the so-called Sirlin function~\cite{Sirlin:1967zz},
L000395	up to a constant  that depends on the choice of ultraviolet (UV) regulator and is fixed by the matching procedure.
L000396	Consistently with the explicit calculation of the neutron decay
L000397	\cite{Cirigliano:2022hob,Cirigliano:2023fnz} we find for the pion $\beta$ decay
L000398	\begin{align}
L000399	\frac{d \Gamma}{d \Gamma^{(0)} } & =  [g_V^\pi (\mu_\chi)]^2
L000400	\left[ 1 + \frac{\alpha}{2 \pi} \bigg( \frac{5}{4}  + 3 \log \frac{\mu_\chi}{m_e} + \hat g(E_e, E_0)   \bigg) \right]
L000401	\nonumber \\
L000402	& = 1 + 2 \, \bar\Box^V_\pi(\mu_0)
L000403	+ \frac{\alpha}{2 \pi} \bigg(
L000404	4 \Big(1-\frac{\alpha_s(\mu_0)}{4\pi}\Big)\log\frac{M_Z}{\mu_0}
L000405	\nonumber \\
L000406	&+ 3 \log \frac{\mu_0}{m_e} + \hat g(E_e, E_0)  \bigg) +
L000407	\Order(\alpha^2, \alpha \alpha_s) ,
L000408	\label{eq:rate0}
L000409	\end{align}
L000410	where in the second line we have provided the fixed-order expression
L000411	(up to $\Order (\alpha \alpha_s)$ terms that are not logarithmically enhanced)
L000412	and  $\hat g(E_e,E_0)$ is the ``subtracted'' Sirlin function~\cite{Sirlin:1967zz},
L000413	from which the large logarithm of $\Mhad/m_e$ has been subtracted:
L000414	\begin{align}
L000415	\label{Sirlin}
L000416	\hat g (E_e, E_0)  & = - \frac{3}{4}
L000417	+ \bigg[\frac{1 + \beta^2}{\beta}
L000418	+ \frac{1}{12 \beta} \bigg( \frac{\bar E}{E_e}\bigg)^2\bigg]  \log \frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}
L000419	\nonumber \\
L000420	&+4 \left[ \frac{1}{2 \beta} \log \frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta} - 1 \right] \left[ \log \frac{2 \bar E}{m_e}  - \frac{3}{2} + \frac{\bar E}{3 E_e} \right]
L000421	\nonumber \\
L000422	&+
L000423	\frac{1}{\beta} \left[ - 4 \,  \text{Li}_2 \left( \frac{2 \beta}{1 + \beta} \right) - \log^2 \left( \frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta} \right)   \right].
L000424	\end{align}
L000425	Here  $\beta = |\mathbf{p}_e|/E_e$ and $\bar E = E_0 - E_e$, with the electron endpoint energy  $E_0$, which, to this order, is the only source through which the hadron mass  enters the radiative corrections.
L000426	The ChPT results~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng} capture additional $\mpi$-dependent nonuniversal corrections, but the integral over the Sirlin function,
L000427	\begin{align}
L000428	\Delta I_{\pi\ell}&\simeq \frac{\alpha}{2\pi}\bigg[\frac{5}{4}+3\log\frac{2E_0}{m_e}\notag\\
L000429	&\qquad+\frac{64}{\mpi^5I_{\pi\ell}}\int_{m_e}^{E_0}dE_e\,E_e^2\bar E^2 \beta \,\hat g (E_e, E_0)\bigg]\notag\\
L000430	&\simeq\frac{\alpha}{\pi}\bigg(\frac{187}{40}-\frac{2\pi^2}{3}\bigg)\simeq -0.0044,
L000431	\end{align}
L000432	explains the main features of the phase-space correction $\Delta I_{\pi\ell}$. In particular, the last line, applicable in the limit $m_e\to 0$ and neglecting recoil corrections, demonstrates that with the choice $\log\frac{\mu_\chi}{2E_0}$ in Eq.~\eqref{Delta_RC_def} indeed all logarithms disappear in $\Delta I_{\pi\ell}$~\cite{Passera:2011ae}.
L000433	
L000434	
L000435	\emph{Low-energy RG}---The low-energy RG for $X_\ell(\mu_\chi,\mu,a)$ needs to be formulated at the level of $g_V^\pi(\mu_\chi)$ to account for the evolution of $\alpha_\chi(\mu_\chi)$, leading to
L000436	\begin{align}
L000437	\label{gvpi_RG}
L000438	\mu_\chi\frac{dg_V^\pi(\mu_\chi)}{d\mu_\chi}&=\gamma(\alpha_\chi)g_\pi^V(\mu_\chi),\notag\\
L000439	\gamma(\alpha_\chi)&=\tilde\gamma_0\frac{\alpha_\chi}{4\pi}+\tilde\gamma_1\frac{\alpha^2_\chi}{(4\pi)^2}+\Order\big(\alpha_\chi^3\big),\notag\\
L000440	\tilde\gamma_0&=-3,\qquad\tilde\gamma_1=\frac{10\tilde n}{3}+\frac{5}{2}-\frac{8\pi^2}{3},
L000441	\end{align}
L000442	where
L000443	\beq
L000444	\tilde n(\mu_\chi)=\sum_{\ell=e,\mu} Q_\ell^2 n_\ell\theta\big(\mu_\chi-m_\ell\big)+\frac{1}{4}\sum_\pi Q_\pi^2 n_\pi\theta\big(\mu_\chi-M_\pi)
