L000527	Introducing the weak vector and axial-vector currents as $V_\mu = \bar u \gamma_\mu d$ and $A_\mu = \bar u \gamma_\mu \gamma_5 d$,
L000528	the Fermi  amplitude  for the process
L000529	$h_i (p_i ) \to h_f (p_f) + e (p_e) + \bar \nu_e (p_\nu)$
L000530	at tree level reads
L000531	\beq
L000532	A_0 = - \sqrt{2} G_F V_{ud}
L000533	C_\beta^{(3)}
L000534	J_\text{lept}^\mu  \langle h_f (p_f) | V_\mu (0) | h_i (p_i) \rangle,
L000535	\label{eq:app1}
L000536	\eeq
L000537	where $J_\text{lept}^\mu  =  \bar u(p_e) \gamma^\mu P_L v (p_\nu)$.
L000538	The following analysis assumes that the hadronic states $h_{i,f}$ are
L000539	 members of the same isospin $I$ multiplet (characterized by the mass scale $\Mhad$):
L000540	 this holds for neutron decay ($I=1/2$),  pion decay ($I=1$), and $0^+ \to 0^+$  nuclear decays ($I=1$).
L000541	 Therefore,  the mass splitting, the  momentum transfer $q = p_i - p_f$, and
L000542	 the electron endpoint energy $E_0$ are all quantities of first order in IB,
L000543	 $\Order\big(\alpha, (m_u-m_d)/\Lambda_\text{QCD}\big)$.
L000544	In terms of the correlators   of the electromagnetic and weak vector or axial-vector currents ($W=V,A$),\footnote{To ensure consistency with the original references~\cite{Descotes-Genon:2005wrq,Abers:1968zz}, the conventions for the correlator in Eqs.~\eqref{TVV_corr} and~\eqref{eq:app2} are kept different, related by $k\to -k$ and an exponential $e^{i(p_f-p_i)\cdot x}$ due to translational invariance.}
L000545	\begin{align}
L000546	W^{\mu \nu} (k) &= i \int d^d x  \,  e^{-i k \cdot x} \notag\\  &\times\big\langle h_f (p_f) \big|  \, T  \big\{W^\mu (0) J_\text{em}^\nu (x) \big\}  \big| h_i (p_i) \big\rangle,
L000547	\label{eq:app2}
L000548	\end{align}
L000549	including the $\Order (\alpha)$ electromagnetic corrections depicted in
L000550	Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams},
L000551	the LEFT  $\beta$ decay amplitude
L000552	$A = - 2  \sqrt{2} G_F V_{ud}
L000553	C_\beta^{(3)} \,   \langle \bar \nu_e e  h_f  | O_\beta  | h_i \rangle$
L000554	 can be written in Feynman gauge as
L000555	 \begin{align}
L000556	 A & = A_0 \Bigg[ \sqrt{Z_e}  - 2 e^2  i \int \frac{d^dk}{(2 \pi)^d} \frac{1}{(k^2 + i \epsilon) (\tilde k^2-m_e^2 + i \epsilon)} \Bigg]\notag\\
L000557	&+
L000558	   \int \frac{d^dk}{(2 \pi)^d} \frac{2e^2i \sqrt{2} G_F V_{ud} C^{(3)}_\beta   J^\mu_\text{lept} p_e^\nu \bar V_{\mu\nu}(k)
L000559	 }{ (k^2 + i \epsilon) (\tilde k^2-m_e^2 + i \epsilon)}
L000560	 \nonumber \\
L000561	 & + \sqrt{2} G_F V_{ud} C^{(3)}_\beta  \bar u(p_e) \frac{ \gamma^\nu \gamma^\alpha \gamma^\mu - \gamma^\mu \gamma^\alpha \gamma^\nu}{2}  P_L  v (p_\nu)\notag\\
L000562	&\times
L000563	  e^2  \int \frac{d^dk}{(2 \pi)^d} \frac{ k_\alpha  A_{\mu \nu}  (k) }{ (k^2 + i \epsilon)^2 (\tilde k^2-m_e^2 + i \epsilon)},
L000564	  \label{eq:app3}
L000565	\end{align}
L000566	where $\tilde k=k+p_e$ and $\bar V_{\mu\nu}(k)=V_{\mu \nu} (k)
L000567	+ V^\alpha_\alpha (k)  \frac{k_\mu k_\nu}{k^2}$.
L000568	Throughout, we use dimensional regularization with anticommuting $\gamma_5$,
L000569	and use modified minimal subtraction   $\overline{\text{MS}}$  to remove the UV divergences.
L000570	Apart from this,   the derivation of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} completely parallels the analysis of Ref.~\cite{Abers:1968zz},
L000571	which was performed in the $V-A$ theory of weak interactions, i.e., LEFT at operator dimension six.
L000572	Reference~\cite{Abers:1968zz} heavily relied on Ward identities deriving from current algebra~\cite{Abers:1968zz,Sirlin:1977sv},
L000573	which also hold in  dimensionally regulated LEFT.
L000574	$Z_e$ denotes the electron wavefunction renormalization depicted by diagram $(c)$ of Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams}.
L000575	The remaining terms represent contributions from diagrams $(a)$ and $(b)$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams}:
L000576	diagram $(b)$ leads to  the second term in the first line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} (after using the
L000577	Ward identity $k_\mu V^{\nu \mu} = k_\mu V^{\mu\nu} = \langle h_f | V^\nu | h_i \rangle + \Order (\alpha, q/\Mhad)$),
L000578	the term involving $V_{\mu \nu}(k)$  in the second line of  Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3},
L000579	and all the remaining terms involving $A_{\mu \nu}(k)$;
L000580	upon using the Ward identity relating three- and two-current correlation functions~\cite{Abers:1968zz},
L000581	diagram $(a)$ combines with one term in diagram $(b)$, resulting in
L000582	the term involving $V^\alpha_\alpha (k)$ in the second line of  Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3}.
L000583	Finally,  following  Ref.~\cite{Abers:1968zz}  we explicitly
L000584	identified the contributions from  the vector and axial-vector current,
L000585	and note that only the isoscalar electromagnetic current contributes in the integration over $A_{\mu \nu} (k)$.
L000586	
L000587	
L000588	
L000589	We next  discuss the  matrix element  $\langle \bar \nu_e e  h_f  | O_\beta  | h_i \rangle$
L000590	in the  $\overline{\text{MS}}$  scheme.
L000591	The first line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} presents no difficulty.
L000592	For the contributions from the vector correlation function $V_{\mu \nu}$,
L000593	as observed in Refs.~\cite{Abers:1968zz,Sirlin:1977sv},  the second line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} will only contribute terms unsuppressed by
L000594	$E_e/\Mhad$  or $|\mathbf{p}_e|/\Mhad$ if  the correlator  has an IR singularity of the type $V_{\mu \nu} (k)  \propto 1/k$, which in turn can only arise from ``elastic'' intermediate
L000595	states.   Hence, the form of $V_{\mu \nu}$ that provides the desired correction is uniquely fixed  in terms of known matrix elements.
L000596	References~\cite{Abers:1968zz, Sirlin:1977sv}  and the ChPT analyses of pion and neutron $\beta$ decay~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng,Cirigliano:2022hob}
L000597	use slightly different forms for $V_{\mu \nu}(k)$, which, however, all share
L000598	the universal  IR singularity, irrespective of hadron spin:
L000599	\beq
L000600	V^\text{IR}_{\mu \nu} (k)
L000601	 =    \langle h_f (p_f) | V_\mu (0) | h_i (p_i) \rangle \ \frac{v_\nu}{ v \cdot k}  +  O(k^0),
L000602	\eeq
L000603	where $v_\nu$ is the four-velocity of the charged hadron involved in the $\beta$ decay.
L000604	We have written this in a form that makes it clear that the hadron mass  (or spin) does not play any role in these  leading corrections.
L000605	The loops evaluated with the universal term in $V^\text{IR}_{\mu \nu} (k)$ are UV divergent and the finite parts
L000606	defined in  $\overline{\text{MS}}$,
L000607	 when combined with the leading Low contribution to real photon
L000608	emission, lead to the usual Sirlin function~\cite{Sirlin:1967zza} up to a constant term.
L000609	The terms  of  $\Order(k^0)$  lead to corrections proportional to $|\mathbf{p}_e|/\Mhad$, usually neglected.\footnote{They are kept in the full  relativistic loop
L000610	function in the ChPT analysis of pion $\beta$ decay~\cite{Cirigliano:2002ng}.}
L000611	
L000612	
L000613	
L000614	
L000615	
L000616	The treatment of the contributions  from  the axial-vector  weak current and  isoscalar electromagnetic current
L000617	($A_{\mu \nu}$) is more subtle, as this term  involves evanescent structures (as seen from Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3})
L000618	and receives $\Order(\alpha_s)$ corrections~\cite{Sirlin:1981ie,Gorbahn:2025ssv}.
L000619	In order to make contact with nonperturbative input from lattice QCD or  dispersive calculations,
L000620	in the context of neutron decay Refs.~\cite{Cirigliano:2023fnz,Gorbahn:2025ssv} introduced an intermediate renormalization
L000621	scheme that  subtracts  the large-momentum part of the photon loop integral
L000622	involving  $A_{\mu \nu}$,  controlled by the OPE.
L000623	This amounts to replacing the last two lines in  Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3}
L000624	with the  UV convergent quantity
L000625	\begin{align}
L000626	 &\sqrt{2} G_F V_{ud} C^{(3)}_\beta
L000627	  \epsilon^{\nu \alpha \mu \rho}         J_\rho^\text{lept}\notag\\
L000628	  &\times
L000629	  e^2 \int \frac{d^4 k}{(2 \pi)^4} \frac{ k_\alpha  \big[ A_{\mu \nu}  (k) - A_{\mu \nu}^\text{OPE} (k, \mu_0) \big] }{ (k^2 + i \epsilon)^2 (\tilde k^2-m_e^2 + i \epsilon)},
L000630	\end{align}
L000631	in which the Dirac algebra has  been performed in $d=4$ dimensions.
L000632	This scheme also  introduces the arbitrary scale $\mu_0$  in the OPE subtraction~\cite{Cirigliano:2023fnz},
L000633	which serves as a factorization scale and regulates IR divergences.
L000634	The crucial final step is to connect this finite operator to the  $\overline{\text{MS}}$ operator,
L000635	which  requires an additional finite and evanescent-scheme dependent renormalization.
L000636	This scheme-matching conversion was worked out to $\Order (\alpha)$ in Ref.~\cite{Cirigliano:2023fnz}
L000637	and to  $\Order (\alpha \alpha_s)$ in Ref.~\cite{Gorbahn:2025ssv}, to which we refer for further details.
L000638	
L000639	
L000640	Having studied the amplitude in LEFT, we are now ready to match to ChPT.
L000641	Based on the above discussion,  one can see  that  the ChPT amplitude (for any process, such as   pion or neutron decay)   coincides  with the
L000642	first two lines of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3}, up to corrections of order $|\mathbf{p}_e|/\Mhad$, irrelevant at the order at which we work.
L000643	In fact,  the machinery of  ChPT builds up $V_{\mu \nu}$ in terms of low-lying states, thus capturing the leading IR behavior,
L000644	and incorporates the Ward identities, so that the ChPT  loops must respect the form of  Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3}.\footnote{In the case of neutron decay,
L000645	the second term in the first line of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} and part of the second line correspond to the  vertex correction (triangle diagram).
L000646	The proton wavefunction renormalization is included in the $V^\alpha_\alpha$ term in the second line.
L000647	Similar arguments apply to pion $\beta$ decay. In that case, more diagrams  appear in ChPT due to a $\gamma W$ tadpole needed to satisfy the
L000648	Ward identities. When everything is expressed in terms of $V_{\mu \nu}$, the expressions match.}
L000649	If one  used the same regularization and subtraction  scheme  in LEFT and ChPT amplitudes, the  first two lines of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3}
L000650	would be identical in the two theories and contribute nothing to the matching.
L000651	Since indeed one uses different dimensional-regularization   scales in the two theories ($\mu$ and $\mu_\chi$) and the
L000652	two minimal-subtraction schemes differ by a constant ($\overline{\text{MS}}$ versus  $\overline{\text{MS}}_\chi$),
L000653	there is a (trivial) contribution to the matching from the IR loops.
L000654	In the case of neutron decay this contribution to the matching leads to
L000655	$-X_6/2 + 2 (V_1 + V_2) - g_9 = (3/2)/(4\pi)^2 (1 - \log (\mu_\chi^2/\mu^2))$~\cite{Cirigliano:2023fnz}.
L000656	Similarly, in the case of pion decay we obtain  $-\hat X_6/2 -2 (\hat X_2 - K_{12}) = (3/2)/(4\pi)^2 (1 - \log (\mu_\chi^2/\mu^2))$.
L000657	
L000658	On the other hand, the integrals involving the axial-vector current
L000659	(usually referred to as the ``$\gamma W$ box'' contribution, even though at low energy
L000660	after integrating out the $W$ boson  the box reduces to a triangle diagram)
L000661	are not dominated by IR scales and in fact there is no corresponding term  in the ChPT loops---they correspond to local operators in ChPT.
L000662	Therefore, the $A_{\mu \nu}$ terms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app3} contribute to the matching both a finite nonperturbative term and
L000663	a scheme-dependent perturbative term.
L000664	In the case of neutron decay the $\gamma W$ box contribution is encoded
L000665	in the combination of LECs $2(V_3 + V_4)$ (see  Eq.~(73) of Ref.~\cite{Cirigliano:2023fnz}),
L000666	while for pion decay it is encoded by the combination $-(2/3) (X_1 +  \hat X_1)$
L000667	(see Eq.~\eqref{eq:LECs_separate}).
L000668	
L000669	The arguments sketched above lead to the  universal form for the matching condition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:final_res}
L000670	and  the universal shift to the decay rate given in  Eq.~\eqref{eq:rate0},
L000671	which agree with the previous results obtained in the specific case of neutron decay~\cite{Cirigliano:2022hob,Cirigliano:2023fnz,Gorbahn:2025ssv}.
